# Cursor Rules for EM_interp Project

## Project Context
This is an AI safety research project focused on interpretability and alignment. The codebase contains tools for:
- Model fine-tuning and evaluation
- Activation analysis and steering
- Response generation and evaluation
- LoRA analysis and visualization

## Tech Stack
- Python with PyTorch and Transformers
- Hugging Face models and tokenizers
- Jupyter notebook-style cell execution (# %%)
- Pandas for data manipulation
- Various evaluation and analysis tools

## Recent Changes
- Modified `em_interp/eval/MASK/gen_mask_responses.py` to use local Hugging Face models instead of API calls
- Converted the file to notebook format with # %% cell dividers
- Removed async/await patterns and API dependencies
- Added local model loading and generation functions
- Created `em_interp/eval/MASK/steer_and_generate.py` for steering-based generation using l24_data_diff_vector
- **REFACTORED**: Eliminated redundant code by creating `em_interp/eval/MASK/mask_generation_util.py` with shared utilities
- **REFACTORED**: Both main files now use the shared `process_dataframe_generic()` function

## Lessons Learned
- Use `transformers.models.auto.modeling_auto.AutoModelForCausalLM` instead of `transformers.AutoModelForCausalLM`
- Use `transformers.models.auto.tokenization_auto.AutoTokenizer` instead of `transformers.AutoTokenizer`
- Local model generation requires proper chat template application and tokenization
- Steering vectors can be loaded from pre-computed activation differences between misaligned and aligned models
- **REFACTORING**: Use shared utility functions to eliminate code duplication between similar files

